From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 06:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] bls: List the Boot Loader Specification entries of main
 storage

Booting an installed system needs its boot entries, and so far the host
parsed GPT, FAT and the entries itself through block reads over the
proxy. Let m1n1 read them: it finds the one XBOOTLDR partition, opens its
FAT file system with the chainload readers, parses
/loader/entries/*.conf (Type #1) and checks each entry against the disk:
exactly one linux (an arm64 Image or gzip) and devicetree (a DTB, named
as the board's when the caller says which), every initrd present,
root=PARTUUID= naming an arm64 root partition on this disk, no efi, uki
or devicetree-overlay. Entries are sorted as systemd-boot sorts them,
with the UAPI version comparison; boot counters are dropped from the id.

P_BOOT_ENTRIES returns the result as text in BLS syntax: a "contract 1"
line, so format changes show without new ops, then per entry an
"entry ID" line, its BLS keys in file order, "root PARTUUID" and a
"problem TEXT" line per reason it can't boot. Keys BLS doesn't define
are left out, so none can pose as those lines. A disk that can't be read
gives one "error TEXT" line. The caller initialises main storage first;
P_BOOT_ENTRIES_FREE releases the text. The ops are Orchard-local: Asahi
and Hoolock end at 0x14xx, and they get proper numbers upstream. The list also goes to the console.

Unit tests build a GPT and FAT32 image in memory.

Assisted-by: LLM
